How does chronic stress affect the functionality of natural killer cells in the immune system and what is the underlying mechanism responsible for this effect?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","Chronic stress can impair the function of natural killer (NK) cells in the immune system. NK cells are a type of white blood cell that play a crucial role in the immune system by recognizing and killing abnormal cells, such as tumor cells and virus-infected cells. They are part of the innate immune system, which means they can respond to threats without prior exposure. Chronic stress can lead to a decrease in NK cell activity, making the immune system more susceptible to infections and potentially increasing the risk of cancer.

NK cell dysfunction in response to chronic stress is primarily mediated by the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis and the sympathetic nervous system (SNS), which are the body's primary stress response systems. NK cells are also influenced by the immune system itself, such as by the activation of pro-inflammatory cytokines.

In response to stress, the HPA axis releases cortisol, a glucocorticoid hormone, and the SNS releases catecholamines like adrenaline and noradrenaline. These hormones can directly and indirectly affect NK cell function. Additionally, stress can lead to an increase in pro-inflammatory cytokines, which can also modulate NK cell activity.

\boxed{\text{Chronic stress impairs NK cell function through the following mechanisms:}}",relevance,"# Task Description
