"If the loading dose of phenytoin in children is 20 mg/kg of body weight to be infused at a rate of 0.5 mg/kg/min, over how many minutes should the dose be administered to a 32-lb. child?","<bos><start_of_turn>user
If the loading dose of phenytoin in children is 20 mg/kg of body weight to be infused at a rate of 0.5 mg/kg/min, over how many minutes should the dose be administered to a 32-lb. child?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","1. **Convert the child's weight to kilograms (kg):
   \[
   \text{Weight in kg} = \text{Weight in lb} \times \frac{1 \text{ kg}}{2.2 \text{ lb}} = 32 \text{ lb} \times \frac{1 \text{ kg}}{2.2 \text{ lb}} = 14.8 \text{ kg}
   \]

2. **Calculate the loading dose in mg:
   \[
   \text{Loading dose in mg} = \text{Weight in kg} \times \text{Loading dose per kg} = 14.8 \text{ kg} \times 20 \text{ mg/kg} = 298 \text{ mg}
   \]

3. **Calculate the infusion rate in mg/min:
   \[
   \text{Infusion rate in mg/min} = \text{Weight in kg} \times \text{Infusion rate per kg/min} = 14.8 \text{ kg} \times 0.5 \text{ mg/kg/min} = 7.4 \text{ mg/min}
   \]

4. **Calculate the time required for infusion in minutes:
   \[
   \text{Time in minutes} = \frac{\text{Loading dose in mg}}{\text{Infusion rate in mg/min}} = \frac{298 \text{ mg}}{7.4 \text{ mg/min}} \approx 40.26 \text{ minutes}
   \]

Rounding to the nearest minute, the dose should be administered over \(\boxed{40}\) minutes.",fluency,"# Task Description
