Calculate the standard enthalpy change for the reaction between calcium oxide (CaO) and water (H2O) to form calcium hydroxide (Ca(OH)2), given that all species are in their standard state and that the balanced chemical equation is:

CaO(s) + H2O(l) → Ca(OH)2(s)

The standard enthalpies of formation of CaO(s), H2O(l) and Ca(OH)2(s) are -635.1 kJ/mol, -285.8 kJ/mol, and -986.2 kJ/mol respectively.
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The standard enthalpy change for a reaction can be calculated using the Gibbs free energy equation:

ΔH = Σ ΔH₀(products) - Σ ΔH₀(products)

In this case, the balanced equation is:

\[
\text{CaO(s) + H}_2\text{O(l)} → \text{Ca(OH)}_2\text{(s)}
\]

The standard enthalpy of formation (ΔH₀) is given for each species:
- ΔH₀(CaO(s)) = -635.1 kJ/mol
- ΔH₀(H₂O(l)) = -285.8 kJ/mol
- ΔH₀(Ca(OH)₂(s)) = -986.2 kJ/mol

Substitute these values into the equation:

\[
\Delta H = \Delta H₀(\text{Ca(OH)}_2\text{(s)}) + 1 \times \Delta H₀(\text{H}_2\text{O(l)]) - [\Delta H₀(\text{CaO(s)]) + 1 \times \Delta H₀(\text{H}_2\text{(l)])
\]
\[
\Δ H = (-986.2 \, \text{kJ/mol}) + (-285.8 \, \text{kJ/mol}) - (-635.1 \, \text{kg/mol)} - 0
\]
\[
\Delta H = -986.2 - 285.8 + 635.1 = -637.1 \, \text{kJ/mol}
\]

Thus, the standard enthalpy change for the reaction is \(\boxed{-637.1 \, \text{kJ/mol}}\).
