<GROUND_TRUTH>
The configuration of the chiral alcohol product can be determined by measuring its optical activity using a polarimeter. The observed rotation of plane-polarized light is compared to known values for the enantiomers of the alcohol. If the rotation matches the known value for the (R)-enantiomer, the product is (R)-configured, and vice versa for the (S)-enantiomer. This method relies on the fact that enantiomers rotate plane-polarized light equally but in opposite directions.  

For example, if the observed rotation matches the literature value for (R)-2-butanol, the product is confirmed as (R)-configured. $\boxed{\text{The configuration is determined by comparing the measured optical rotation to known values of the enantiomers.}}$
</GROUND_TRUTH>

