","Let $X$ be the number of heads in 12 flips of a fair coin. Since the coin is fair, the probability of heads is $1/2$ and the probability of tails is $1/2$. The number of heads in 12 flips follows a binomial distribution with $n = 12$ and $p = 1/2$. Thus, $X \sim \text{Bin}(12, 1/2)$.
We want to find the probability that we get heads in at least 9 of the 12 flips, which is $P(X \geq 9)$. This is the sum of the probabilities of getting 9, 10, 11, or 12 heads:
\[ P(X \geq 9) = P(X=9) + P(X=10) + P(X=11) + P(X=12) \]
The probability of getting exactly $k$ heads in 12 flips is given by the binomial probability formula:
\[ P(X=k) = \frac{n!}{k! (n-k)!} p^k (1-p)^{n-k} = \frac{12!}{k! (12-k)!} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^k \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{12-k} = \frac{12!}{k! (12-k)!} \leftfrac{1}{2}\right^{12} \]
Thus,
\[ P(X=9) = \frac{12!}{9! 3!} \leftfrac{1}{2}\right^{12} = \frac{12 \cdot 11 \cdot 10}{3 \cdot 2 \cdot 1} \leftfrac{1}{2}\right^{12} = 200 \leftfrac{1}{2}\right^{12} \]
\[ P(X=10) = \frac{12!}{10!  2!} \leftfrac{1}{2}\right^{12} = \frac{12 \cdot 11}{2 \cdot 1} \leftfrac{1}{2}\right^{12} = 66 \leftfrac{1}{2}\right^{12} \]
$$ P(X=11) = \frac{12!}{11! 1!} \leftfrac{1}{2}\right^{12} = 12 \leftfrac{1}{2}\right^{12} \]
\[ P(X=12) = \frac{12!}{12! 0!} \leftfrac{1}{2}\right^{12} = 1 \leftfrac{1}{2}\right^{12} \]
Thus,
\[ P(X \geq 9) = \sum_{k=9}^{12} P(X=k) = \left 200 + 66 + 12 + 1 \right \leftfrac{1}{2}\right^{12} = 279 \leftfrac{1}{2^{12}} = \frac{279}{1024} \]
Thus, the probability is $\frac{279}{1024}$.

Final Answer:
\[ \frac{279}{1024} \approx 0.267 \]

Final Answer: $\boxed{279/1024}$",relevance,"# Task Description
