","The equation of the line tangent to the graph of a differentiable function \( f \) at the point \( (a, f(a)) \) is given by the equation of the tangent line at the point \( (a, f(a)) \).  
The equation of the tangent line is given by the equation:  
\[ y - f(a) = \frac{\partial f}{a} ( x - a) \]  
where \( \frac{\partial f}{a} \) is the derivative of \( f \) at \( x = a \), which is denoted as \( f'(a) \).  
Thus, the equation of the tangent line is:  
\[ y - f(a) = f'(a) ( x - a) \]  
This equation represents the line that passes through the point \( (a, f(a) \). The slope of the line is \( f'(a) \), which is the derivative of \( f \) at \( x = a \).  

Thus, the equation of the tangent line is:  
\[ y = f'(a) ( x - a) + f(a) \]  

Final Answer:  
\[ y = f'(a)(x - a) + f(a) \]  

