<start_of_turn>model
","In stratified sampling, the sample is divided into groups (strata) based on the type of dairy product. The number of samples from each stratum is proportional to the size of the stratum.
The total number of brands is:
\[
30 + 10 + 35 + 25 = 100
\]
The number of brands for each type is:
\begin{itemize}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Pure milk: 30 brands
        \item Yogurt: 10 brands
        \item Infant formula: 35 brands
        \item Adult milk powder: 25 brands
    \end{itemize
\end{itemize}
The proportion of each type of dairy product is:
\begin{itemize}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Pure milk: $\frac{30}{100} = 0.3$
        \item Yogurt: \(\frac{10}{100} = 0.1\)
        \item Infant formula: \(\frac{35}{100} = 0.35\)
        \item Adult milk powder: \(\frac{25}{100} = 0.25\)
    \end{itemize
\end{item}
The sample size is \(n\). The number of samples from each type is proportional to the proportion of the type in the total number of brands.
\begin{itemize}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Pure milk: \(0.3n\)
        \item Yogurt: \(0.1n\)
        \item Infant formula: \(0.35n\)
        \item Adult milk powder: \(0.25n\)
    \end{itemize
\end{item}
We are given that 7 brands of infant formula are sampled. Thus, the number of samples from the infant formula type is 7.
\[
0.35n = 7
\]
Solving for \(n\):
\[
N = \frac{7}{0.35} = \frac{7}{35/100} = \frac{7 \cdot 100}{35} = \frac{700}{35} = 20
\]
Thus, the total sample size is \(n = 20\).
The number of samples from each type is:
\begin{itemize}
    \beginitemize}
        \item Pure milk: \(0.3 \times 20 = 6\)
        \item Yogurt: \(0.1 \times 20 = 2\)
        \item Infant formula: \(0.35 \times 20 = 7\)
        \item Adult milk powder: \(0.25 \times 20 = 5\)
    \end{item
    \end{itemize
The sum of the sample sizes is \(6 + 2 + 7 + 5 = 20\), which is the total sample size \(n\).

