<USER_REQUEST>
Calculate the average radiation dose to the liver if 1.6 kg of liver tissue absorbs 0.12 J of energy from a radiopharmaceutical.
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To calculate the average radiation dose to the liver, we use the formula:
\[
\text{Dose} = \frac{\text{Energy absorbed}}{\text{Mass of tissue}}
\]
where:
- Dose is the radiation dose, typically measured in Gy (Gray) or mGy (milligray).
- Energy absorbed is the energy deposited in the tissue, measured in J (Joules).
- Mass of tissue is the mass of the tissue, measured in kg (kilograms).

Given:
- Energy absorbed = 0.12 J
- Mass of liver tissue = 1.6 kg

Substituting the given values into the formula:
\[
\text{Dose} = \frac{0.12 \text{ J}}{1.6 \text{ kg}} = 0.075 \text{ Gy}
\]

Thus, the average radiation dose to the liver is 0.075 Gy.

To express the dose in mGy, we multiply by 1000:
\[
0.075 \text{ Gy} \times 1000 = 75 \text{ mGy}
\]

The average radiation dose to the liver is \(\boxed{75 \text{ mGy}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

