- \( B \) is the magnetic field strength at the center of the coil.
- \( \mu_0 \) is the permeability of free space, which is \( 4 \times 10^{-6} \, \text{T} \cdot \text{A}cdot \text{m}^{-1} \).
- \( N \) is the number of turns in the coil.
- \( I \) is the current flowing through the coil.
- \( R \) is the radius of the coil.

In this case, we are given:
- \( N = 100 \) turns.
- \( I = 1 \, \text{A} \).
- \( R = 0.2 \, \text{m} \).

Substituting these values into the formula:
\[
B = \frac{(4 \times 10^{-6} \, \text{T} \cdot \text{A}cdot \text{m}^{-1}) \times 100 \times 1 \, \text{A}}{2 \times 0.2 \, \text{m}}
\]
\[
B = \frac{4 \times 10^{-6} \times 100}{0.4} \, \text{T}
\]
\[
B = \frac{400 \times 10^{-6}}{0.4} \, \text{T}
\]
\[
B = 1000 \times 10^{-6} \, \text{T}
\]
\[
B = 1 \times 10^{-3} \, \text{T}
\]
Thus, the magnetic field strength at the center of the circular coil is \( 1 \times 10^{-3} \, \text{T} \).

Final Answer:
\[
\boxed{1 \times 10^{-3} \, \text{T}}
\]
