where \( \vec{v}_m \) and \( \vec{v}_M \) are the velocities of the smaller and larger masses after the collision, respectively, and \( \vec{v}_0 \) is the initial velocity of the smaller mass.
The relative velocity after the collision is \( \vec{v}_M - \vec{v}_m \), and the relative velocity before the collision is \( \vec{v}_0 \). The distance between the objects is \( d \). The time \( t \) for the objects to collide again is given by the distance divided by the relative velocity:
\[ t = \frac{d}{\vec{v}_M - \vec{v}_m} = \frac{d}{e \vec{v}_0} \]
If the initial velocity is in the direction of the distance between the objects, then the relative velocity after the collision is \( e v_0 \). The distance between the objects is \( d \), so the time taken for the objects to collide again is:
\[ t = \frac{d}{e v_0} \]

