\[ Q^2 = (P_f - P_i)^2 \]
Substituting the given four-momenta:
\[ Q^2 = (P_F - P_I)^2 = (E_f/c, \mathbf{p}_f) - (E_i/c, \mathbf{p}_i)^2 \]
Using the dot product definition of the square of a four-momentum:
\[ Q^2 = \left( \frac{E_f}{c} - \frac{E_i}{c} \right)^2 + \left ( \mathbf{p}_f - \mathbf{p}_i \right \cdot (\mathbf{p}_f - \mathbf{p}_i) \]
\[ Q^2 = \left( \frac{E_f - E_i}{c} \right)^2 + \left ( \mathbf{p}_f - \mathbf{p}_i \right)^2 \]
Since the electron mass is negligible, the energy of the electron is proportional to its momentum, i.e., \( E = m c^2 = p c \), where \( m \) is the electron mass. Thus, \( E = p c \).
Substituting \( E_i = p_i c \) and \( E_f = p_f c \):
\[ Q^2 = \left( \frac{p_f c - p_i c}{c} \right)^2 + \left ( \mathbf{p}_f - \mathbf{p}_i \right)^2 \]
\[ Q^2 = (p_f - p_i)^2 + \left ( \mathbf{p}_f - \mathbf{i \right)^2 \]
Since \( p_i \) is a scalar, \( (p_f - p_i)^2 = (p_f - p_i)^2 \), and \( (\mathbf{p}_f - \mathbf{p}_i)^2 = p_f^2 + p_i^2 - 2 \mathbf{p}_f \cdot \mathbf{p}_i \).
